<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ifat Sultanate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ifat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ifat Sultanate was a Muslim state in the eastern Ethiopian region, ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walashma dynasty</a> from the late thirteenth century. It came into conflict with the Christian Ethiopian kingdom and was defeated. Its ruling family later re-established power further east, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ifat-sultanate.jpg" alt="Ifat Sultanate" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Skilla1st · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Sultanate_of_Ifat.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Adal Sultanate was founded around 1415 CE by Sabr ad-Din III, a prince of the Walashma dynasty who returned from exile in Yemen after the catastrophic defeat of its predecessor state, the <b>Ifat Sultanate</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Consolidation came under Amda Seyon I, who acceded in 1314, organised the army into standing chewa regiments, and launched expansion southward across the Awash River and eastward, subduing <b>Ifat</b>, Dawaro, Bale, and other Muslim principalities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest account of <b>Ifat</b> is Ibn Sa'id al-Maghribi's: the region was called Jabarta and its capital Wafāt, a city on high ground in a valley beside a river, on the eastern edge of Shewa, whose Muslim inhabitants of mixed descent grew bananas and sugar cane.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-ifat">Sultanate of Ifat · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Arababni is known only from the 14th-century account of al-Umari, who placed it among the Muslim states of Abyssinia, apparently between <b>Ifat</b> and Hadiya.</blockquote><a href="/state/arababni">Arababni</a></li><li><blockquote>Dara is known only from brief mentions, chiefly by the 14th-century Arab writer al-Umari, who listed it among the seven Muslim kingdoms of Abyssinia alongside <b>Ifat</b>, Dawaro, Arababni, Hadiya, Sharkha and Bale.</blockquote><a href="/state/dara-sultanate">Dara Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Dawaro lay south of <b>Ifat</b>, between the Awash and the upper Wabi Shebelle river, and appears in sources from the 13th century as one of the Muslim states of the Ethiopian interior.</blockquote><a href="/state/sultanate-of-dawaro">Sultanate of Dawaro</a></li></ul></section>
